<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aginnum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agen is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lot-et-garonne">Lot-et-Garonne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. As Aginnum it was the chief town of the Gallic Nitiobroges, and in 107 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigurini">Tigurini</a> defeated a Roman army in its region. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a bishopric and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agenais">Agenais</a>, which passed between the English and French crowns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agen.jpg" alt="Agen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KoS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_Agen_3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Agen comes from Occitan Agen, recorded in 1197, itself from Latin Aginnum, which appears in the Itinéraire d'Antonin of the 3rd century. It derives from a Celtic root agin- meaning rock or height. Aginnum was the capital of the Celtic tribe of the Nitiobroges, and extensive Roman ruins attest its importance under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. Its bishopric was founded in the 4th century, and Saint Caprasius of Agen, a Christian martyr, and the bishop Phoebadius of Agen, who died around 392, belong to that century.</p><p>Agen changed hands more than once in the course of the Albigensian wars. At their close a tribunal of inquisition was established in the town and inflicted cruel persecution on the heretics. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> settled there in the twelfth century. They were expelled in 1306, a number returned in 1315, and a Rue des Juifs has been documented since then. During the religious wars of the 16th century Agen took the part of the Catholics and openly joined the League in 1589. The Hôtel de Ville was completed around 1666.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Agen is the prefecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lot-et-garonne">Lot-et-Garonne</a>, on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> in the region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine, about 135 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>. The Canal des Deux Mers, joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> with the Atlantic, crosses the river there on a canal bridge. Agen Cathedral, dedicated to Saint Caprasius, dates from the twelfth century and is one of the few large churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with a double nave. The art museum holds works by Goya, Bonnard and Seurat.</p><p>Louis Arthur Ducos du Hauron, a pioneer of colour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a>, lived and worked in Agen. He patented his three-colour principle in 1868 and published it in 1869, and his View of Agen of 1877 is the most widely reproduced of his surviving colour photographs. Agen is called the capital of the prune, a local product eaten stuffed or in pastries or soaked in Armagnac. The population of the commune was 32,801 in 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bishoprics were created at Albi around 1165, then at Toulouse, Carcassonne and <b>Agen</b>, four by 1200, and Razès about 1225.</blockquote><a href="/religion/catharism">Catharism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>They were divided into four cantons, and one of them, the Tigurini under Divico, joined the Cimbri and defeated a Roman army near <b>Agen</b> in 107 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvetii">Helvetii</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nitiobroges lived on the Garonne around <b>Aginnum</b> (Agen), at the border between the Celtic peoples and the Aquitani.</blockquote><a href="/state/nitiobroges">Nitiobroges</a></li></ul></section>
